WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.712
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:00:06.105 --> 00:00:07.980
KARTHI THYAGARAJAN: I'm
a solutions architect

00:00:07.980 --> 00:00:10.230
with Google Cloud.

00:00:10.230 --> 00:00:15.180
And to start off with, I'm
going to talk about what a data

00:00:15.180 --> 00:00:18.090
lake is, how you would
structure your data

00:00:18.090 --> 00:00:21.930
lake on GCP with a lot
of help from BigQuery,

00:00:21.930 --> 00:00:24.750
and then after that,
Dhaval Durve over there,

00:00:24.750 --> 00:00:28.460
who is a strategic cloud
engineer with Google Cloud,

00:00:28.460 --> 00:00:32.610
will talk about how you'd
operationalize BigQuery.

00:00:32.610 --> 00:00:34.550
And then lastly,
Tomasz Stradomski

00:00:34.550 --> 00:00:37.950
who is also over there,
will go into detail

00:00:37.950 --> 00:00:41.190
about how Ocado Technology--
by the way, Tomasz

00:00:41.190 --> 00:00:43.710
leads up data
engineering at Ocado.

00:00:43.710 --> 00:00:46.770
He'll talk about how
Ocado built out a data

00:00:46.770 --> 00:00:50.600
lake with a lot of help,
once again, from BigQuery.

00:00:53.389 --> 00:00:55.180
I know we don't have
everybody in the room,

00:00:55.180 --> 00:00:57.610
but I'm going to do a
quick informal survey.

00:00:57.610 --> 00:00:59.530
So by a show of
hands, how many of you

00:00:59.530 --> 00:01:02.050
here have either built
out a data lake already,

00:01:02.050 --> 00:01:04.912
or are thinking about
building out a data lake?

00:01:04.912 --> 00:01:06.250
OK, I'm not surprised.

00:01:06.250 --> 00:01:08.350
That's good.

00:01:08.350 --> 00:01:14.560
So this term, data lake,
is thrown out a lot.

00:01:14.560 --> 00:01:17.710
It has a lot of meanings to
a lot of different people.

00:01:17.710 --> 00:01:20.350
What I'd like to do first
is do some level setting

00:01:20.350 --> 00:01:23.770
on what I mean by data lake for
the purposes of this session.

00:01:23.770 --> 00:01:27.350
And I think you'll agree with
most of these characteristics.

00:01:27.350 --> 00:01:32.020
The first one is this philosophy
of storing everything.

00:01:32.020 --> 00:01:34.660
And what that
recognizes is the fact

00:01:34.660 --> 00:01:37.630
that we want to guide
the questions we're

00:01:37.630 --> 00:01:41.470
asking of our data based on
the data that we can collect.

00:01:41.470 --> 00:01:43.250
We want to collect
everything, of course,

00:01:43.250 --> 00:01:48.970
within compliance restrictions
and storage restrictions,

00:01:48.970 --> 00:01:52.170
but yes, we do want
to collect everything.

00:01:52.170 --> 00:01:55.010
And once we collect
the data, our data lake

00:01:55.010 --> 00:01:58.010
has to facilitate exploration.

00:01:58.010 --> 00:02:01.670
That's how we go from raw
data to refined data products

00:02:01.670 --> 00:02:05.350
that will inform us, whether
it's about business decisions,

00:02:05.350 --> 00:02:08.090
you know, new opportunities,
operational efficiencies,

00:02:08.090 --> 00:02:09.720
things of that nature.

00:02:09.720 --> 00:02:12.530
And once we surface
these data products,

00:02:12.530 --> 00:02:16.190
we want to be able to
bring a variety of tools

00:02:16.190 --> 00:02:18.840
to process these data products.

00:02:18.840 --> 00:02:20.590
Of course, this session
is about BigQuery,

00:02:20.590 --> 00:02:24.190
so it'll feature prominently
in how you do analytics,

00:02:24.190 --> 00:02:27.610
but in addition, we want to
be able to allow our data

00:02:27.610 --> 00:02:31.720
scientists to use
Spark, MapReduce, Cloud

00:02:31.720 --> 00:02:34.420
Dataflow, which is our
serverless processing

00:02:34.420 --> 00:02:36.280
engine, all of these tools.

00:02:39.570 --> 00:02:42.430
So with those
characteristics in mind,

00:02:42.430 --> 00:02:47.070
here's a high level flow of
how things go from raw data

00:02:47.070 --> 00:02:48.501
to the refined data products.

00:02:48.501 --> 00:02:50.250
And this will be kind
of a theme, at least

00:02:50.250 --> 00:02:52.410
during my presentation.

00:02:52.410 --> 00:02:55.050
On this slide, we're going to
focus on the ingest and store

00:02:55.050 --> 00:02:56.400
piece.

00:02:56.400 --> 00:02:58.390
We ingest data from
a variety of sources.

00:02:58.390 --> 00:03:01.410
We want to facilitate
batch analytics as well

00:03:01.410 --> 00:03:02.910
as stream analytics.

00:03:02.910 --> 00:03:06.150
And when it comes time
to store the data,

00:03:06.150 --> 00:03:08.250
I'm going to say
right off the bat

00:03:08.250 --> 00:03:12.360
that Google Cloud Storage,
also known as GCS,

00:03:12.360 --> 00:03:14.272
is the ideal choice
for the lake itself.

00:03:14.272 --> 00:03:15.730
And you're probably
wondering, hey,

00:03:15.730 --> 00:03:19.590
this is a session on BigQuery,
why is he talking about GCS.

00:03:19.590 --> 00:03:22.290
And that's because--
and by the way,

00:03:22.290 --> 00:03:25.320
when Tomasz goes into detail
over how they built out

00:03:25.320 --> 00:03:27.510
their data lake, you'll
hear that they're

00:03:27.510 --> 00:03:30.210
ingesting structured data,
storing it directly in BigQuery

00:03:30.210 --> 00:03:32.092
and doing analytics
with BigQuery.

00:03:32.092 --> 00:03:32.800
And that's great.

00:03:32.800 --> 00:03:35.460
We love that.

00:03:35.460 --> 00:03:36.820
Two things to keep in mind.

00:03:36.820 --> 00:03:40.770
The first one is that as
your data volume grows,

00:03:40.770 --> 00:03:42.840
as you go from
hundreds of terabytes

00:03:42.840 --> 00:03:45.330
to say many, many
petabtyes of data,

00:03:45.330 --> 00:03:48.270
it makes financial sense
to lifecycle your older

00:03:48.270 --> 00:03:51.960
data from BigQuery into one of
the archival storage classes

00:03:51.960 --> 00:03:53.970
that GCS has.

00:03:53.970 --> 00:03:58.860
This way, you can balance
cost as well as analytics

00:03:58.860 --> 00:04:01.110
capability.

00:04:01.110 --> 00:04:03.564
And then the other
reason is that you

00:04:03.564 --> 00:04:05.730
want to be able to do the
flexible processing that I

00:04:05.730 --> 00:04:06.870
talked about.

00:04:06.870 --> 00:04:09.630
SQL is great, but once again,
you want to bring in Spark,

00:04:09.630 --> 00:04:11.130
you want to bring in MapReduce.

00:04:11.130 --> 00:04:13.200
You want to do
predictive analytics.

00:04:13.200 --> 00:04:15.144
And for that, GCS is great.

00:04:15.144 --> 00:04:17.310
It's a great central
repository that integrates well

00:04:17.310 --> 00:04:20.730
with GCS services,
as you'll find out,

00:04:20.730 --> 00:04:23.280
as well as a whole slew
of third party services.

00:04:26.310 --> 00:04:29.310
And I mentioned, you know,
working well with GCS.

00:04:29.310 --> 00:04:32.070
And BigQuery, as
you would expect,

00:04:32.070 --> 00:04:35.110
works really well GCS as well.

00:04:35.110 --> 00:04:38.340
For example, if you
have Avro files in GCS,

00:04:38.340 --> 00:04:40.470
you can query those
files, you know,

00:04:40.470 --> 00:04:42.780
with rich schema and
all of that good stuff

00:04:42.780 --> 00:04:45.540
directly from BigQuery without
first loading that data

00:04:45.540 --> 00:04:47.340
into BigQuery.

00:04:47.340 --> 00:04:50.490
Now, having said that, for the
vast majority of use cases,

00:04:50.490 --> 00:04:53.474
you actually want to pull
that data into BigQuery first,

00:04:53.474 --> 00:04:55.140
because it gives you
better performance,

00:04:55.140 --> 00:04:58.270
it gives you more native
querying capability.

00:04:58.270 --> 00:05:00.470
And you'll be happy to
find out that import

00:05:00.470 --> 00:05:05.850
and export to and from
GCS into BigQuery is free.

00:05:05.850 --> 00:05:10.890
And the last reason to use
BigQuery and compare it

00:05:10.890 --> 00:05:13.604
in combination with
GCS is that they share

00:05:13.604 --> 00:05:14.770
a compatible security model.

00:05:14.770 --> 00:05:18.730
They both use GCP Identity
and Access Management.

00:05:18.730 --> 00:05:21.530
This has ramifications for
how you do access control,

00:05:21.530 --> 00:05:25.315
as you'll find out from
Dhaval, and encryption as well.

00:05:25.315 --> 00:05:26.940
So if you have a
compliance regime that

00:05:26.940 --> 00:05:32.100
requires you to use customer
managed encryption keys,

00:05:32.100 --> 00:05:35.490
then you can use those same keys
for encrypting and decrypting

00:05:35.490 --> 00:05:37.260
data in both GCS and BigQuery.

00:05:40.660 --> 00:05:42.280
So we talked about ingest.

00:05:42.280 --> 00:05:44.140
Let's get into the other
two characteristics

00:05:44.140 --> 00:05:45.629
that I talked about.

00:05:45.629 --> 00:05:47.170
There's a lot going
on in this slide,

00:05:47.170 --> 00:05:51.170
so I'm going to unpack it into
smaller, digestible pieces.

00:05:51.170 --> 00:05:54.070
And once again, the question
that's going to guide that

00:05:54.070 --> 00:05:56.740
is how do we get from
all this raw data

00:05:56.740 --> 00:06:01.170
that I talked about to
the refined data products.

00:06:01.170 --> 00:06:04.050
To answer that question,
let's break those refined data

00:06:04.050 --> 00:06:06.030
products into three categories.

00:06:06.030 --> 00:06:08.970
These are categories
that I feel, you know,

00:06:08.970 --> 00:06:10.720
will help us guide
this discussion.

00:06:10.720 --> 00:06:13.740
So the first one is this
notion of a data mart.

00:06:13.740 --> 00:06:18.347
This should be familiar to folks
who use SQL data warehousing,

00:06:18.347 --> 00:06:20.430
but I want to expand that
definition a little bit,

00:06:20.430 --> 00:06:23.130
even though this is the thing
that BigQuery is best known

00:06:23.130 --> 00:06:24.630
for, you know, SQL
data warehousing,

00:06:24.630 --> 00:06:26.430
setting up data marts.

00:06:26.430 --> 00:06:28.800
But a lot of
customers these days

00:06:28.800 --> 00:06:31.590
are also doing Parquet
files, Avro files,

00:06:31.590 --> 00:06:35.392
and you know, refining
data in that fashion,

00:06:35.392 --> 00:06:37.350
and exposing them to both
internal and external

00:06:37.350 --> 00:06:38.880
customers.

00:06:38.880 --> 00:06:42.420
If you do that, you can use
Hive, you can use MapReduce,

00:06:42.420 --> 00:06:46.310
you can use Spark on top
of those files as well.

00:06:46.310 --> 00:06:51.120
And you can also use serverless
processing with Cloud Dataflow.

00:06:51.120 --> 00:06:54.810
The second type of data
product is streaming workflows.

00:06:54.810 --> 00:06:58.890
So you know, if you have a
retail e-commerce website,

00:06:58.890 --> 00:07:03.090
and you want to better
understand inventory depletion,

00:07:03.090 --> 00:07:05.910
how customers are
navigating your site,

00:07:05.910 --> 00:07:11.220
you could set up QuickStream
capture, send that

00:07:11.220 --> 00:07:15.480
to Cloud Pub/Sub, which is
our global messaging service,

00:07:15.480 --> 00:07:18.480
and then you could use Cloud
Dataflow to grab messages out

00:07:18.480 --> 00:07:20.520
of Pub/Sub and then
use BigQuery streaming

00:07:20.520 --> 00:07:24.460
API to ingest that data
directly into BigQuery.

00:07:24.460 --> 00:07:28.170
And what that lets you do is
with very low latency query

00:07:28.170 --> 00:07:28.710
that data.

00:07:28.710 --> 00:07:30.750
We're talking a matter
of a few seconds.

00:07:30.750 --> 00:07:36.350
This is pretty powerful and once
again, BigQuery excels at this.

00:07:36.350 --> 00:07:40.040
And the last type of data
product is predictive models.

00:07:40.040 --> 00:07:43.130
So whether it's statistical
models, machine learning

00:07:43.130 --> 00:07:46.550
models, you know, you could
look at the captured raw data,

00:07:46.550 --> 00:07:48.500
figure out what kind
of trends are in there

00:07:48.500 --> 00:07:53.062
and set up a TensorFlow model,
and then import that model

00:07:53.062 --> 00:07:54.770
into Cloud Machine
Learning Engine, which

00:07:54.770 --> 00:07:58.160
is our serverless machine
learning framework,

00:07:58.160 --> 00:08:01.790
and train that with
all the training data

00:08:01.790 --> 00:08:05.180
you've captured using
GPUs, TPUs, and then

00:08:05.180 --> 00:08:08.690
deploy that trained model into
Cloud Machine Learning Engine

00:08:08.690 --> 00:08:12.000
once again and do inference.

00:08:12.000 --> 00:08:14.660
So those are the three
types of data products.

00:08:14.660 --> 00:08:17.290
One thing I skipped is how
you do mining and exploration

00:08:17.290 --> 00:08:19.490
in between.

00:08:19.490 --> 00:08:26.770
So taking all that raw data and
coming up with data products,

00:08:26.770 --> 00:08:28.550
that's in keeping
with our philosophy

00:08:28.550 --> 00:08:32.120
of letting the data guide
the types of questions

00:08:32.120 --> 00:08:33.140
we're going to ask.

00:08:33.140 --> 00:08:35.809
And this has to be
an iterative process.

00:08:35.809 --> 00:08:39.950
We're not going to be kind of
rigid in the types of schemas

00:08:39.950 --> 00:08:42.500
that we will expose
to our analysts.

00:08:42.500 --> 00:08:47.150
In order to do that, you
need an agile, nimble way

00:08:47.150 --> 00:08:47.910
of doing things.

00:08:47.910 --> 00:08:51.570
And this is where BigQuery,
once again, can help a lot.

00:08:51.570 --> 00:08:54.320
So without having to
put a lot of thought

00:08:54.320 --> 00:08:58.040
into the type of schemas
that your raw data has,

00:08:58.040 --> 00:08:59.540
you could load
that into BigQuery,

00:08:59.540 --> 00:09:03.740
set up a BigQuery data set, and
then your data scientists could

00:09:03.740 --> 00:09:06.050
figure out, hey, what
are the types of trends

00:09:06.050 --> 00:09:10.310
that this data has,
how can we surface more

00:09:10.310 --> 00:09:13.100
production ready schemata
in our data marts,

00:09:13.100 --> 00:09:16.730
what type of streaming
workflows can we set up,

00:09:16.730 --> 00:09:19.310
and then also do trend
analysis to figure out

00:09:19.310 --> 00:09:22.310
the kinds of predictive
models we can set up.

00:09:26.350 --> 00:09:28.750
So to wrap things
up for my piece,

00:09:28.750 --> 00:09:30.580
I'd like to go over an
example that kind of

00:09:30.580 --> 00:09:33.770
captures all of this and
also includes orchestration.

00:09:33.770 --> 00:09:39.010
So let's say you have a
data lake that has to pull

00:09:39.010 --> 00:09:41.680
in a lot of unstructured data.

00:09:41.680 --> 00:09:44.620
This is in contrast to something
you'll hear about from Tomasz.

00:09:44.620 --> 00:09:48.180
So let's say you have
videos, images, speech data.

00:09:48.180 --> 00:09:51.470
This is something that has been
opaque up until very recently.

00:09:51.470 --> 00:09:55.750
So what you can do now is
send these types of files

00:09:55.750 --> 00:09:59.590
into one of our serverless
machine learning APIs,

00:09:59.590 --> 00:10:01.060
extract meaningful metadata.

00:10:01.060 --> 00:10:03.400
So if you have a
bunch of cat videos,

00:10:03.400 --> 00:10:05.980
you can send that through
our video intelligence API,

00:10:05.980 --> 00:10:08.680
figure out what types of
objects are in those videos

00:10:08.680 --> 00:10:11.030
and what they're doing,
store that metadata

00:10:11.030 --> 00:10:15.947
in BigQuery along with links
to the actual files themselves.

00:10:15.947 --> 00:10:17.530
And orchestration
is a key piece here,

00:10:17.530 --> 00:10:19.450
because you're pulling
in data on a daily basis,

00:10:19.450 --> 00:10:20.825
and you have to
orchestrate it so

00:10:20.825 --> 00:10:25.375
that metadata magically appears
in a system like BigQuery.

00:10:25.375 --> 00:10:29.170
So Cloud Composer, which is our
managed Apache Airflow service,

00:10:29.170 --> 00:10:31.970
is a great tool to use here.

00:10:31.970 --> 00:10:32.470
Cool.

00:10:32.470 --> 00:10:34.630
So with all of that
said, let's hear

00:10:34.630 --> 00:10:40.472
from Dhaval about how you would
operationalize BigQuery on GCP.

00:10:40.472 --> 00:10:42.384
DHAVAL DURVE: Thank you, Karthi.

00:10:42.384 --> 00:10:46.686
[APPLAUSE]

00:10:48.120 --> 00:10:49.990
Good morning, everyone.

00:10:49.990 --> 00:10:51.750
Welcome to Google Cloud Next.

00:10:51.750 --> 00:10:54.820
My name is Dhaval Durve, and
I'm a strategic cloud engineer

00:10:54.820 --> 00:10:56.520
in Google's cloud team.

00:10:56.520 --> 00:10:59.520
So so far, we have talked
about how BigQuery can be used

00:10:59.520 --> 00:11:01.230
to explore your data in a lake.

00:11:01.230 --> 00:11:02.940
Now let's take one step further.

00:11:02.940 --> 00:11:05.220
Let's try to create a data
model, data warehouse,

00:11:05.220 --> 00:11:09.400
in BigQuery, and walk
through the process for that.

00:11:09.400 --> 00:11:11.160
So how do we
operationalize that?

00:11:11.160 --> 00:11:12.930
Now, first thing in
operationalization

00:11:12.930 --> 00:11:15.030
is how do you ingest
your work flows,

00:11:15.030 --> 00:11:17.400
or how do you build your
ingestion pipelines.

00:11:17.400 --> 00:11:19.510
With BigQuery,
depending on your needs,

00:11:19.510 --> 00:11:21.150
you have many choices there.

00:11:21.150 --> 00:11:23.310
If you want to really
write simple workflows,

00:11:23.310 --> 00:11:26.280
just loading data files, you
can use bq command line tool

00:11:26.280 --> 00:11:27.420
for that.

00:11:27.420 --> 00:11:29.640
If you want to do some
kind of transformations

00:11:29.640 --> 00:11:31.410
but don't want to
do any coding, you

00:11:31.410 --> 00:11:35.230
can use Cloud Dataprep, which
is an excellent tool to do that.

00:11:35.230 --> 00:11:38.640
For more complex transformations
or more flexibility,

00:11:38.640 --> 00:11:40.710
you should use Cloud Dataflow.

00:11:40.710 --> 00:11:42.870
Now, first three
options that I mentioned

00:11:42.870 --> 00:11:45.420
are mainly used to ingest
data from either your batch

00:11:45.420 --> 00:11:48.870
files or either your
middleware systems

00:11:48.870 --> 00:11:51.840
like Kafka or Cloud Pub/Sub.

00:11:51.840 --> 00:11:55.609
If you want to directly ingest
from your application itself,

00:11:55.609 --> 00:11:57.150
BigQuery provides
you a streaming API

00:11:57.150 --> 00:11:59.370
which can be used to
directly ingest data

00:11:59.370 --> 00:12:02.160
from your application
to BigQuery.

00:12:02.160 --> 00:12:04.350
These records which
are ingested are also

00:12:04.350 --> 00:12:08.364
available in near real time
for your users to query.

00:12:08.364 --> 00:12:09.780
So these are some
ways how you can

00:12:09.780 --> 00:12:11.790
ingest your data in BigQuery.

00:12:11.790 --> 00:12:14.640
Now the data is
ingested, so next stop

00:12:14.640 --> 00:12:17.060
is to talk about access control.

00:12:17.060 --> 00:12:20.250
BigQuery takes a really
simple approach over here.

00:12:20.250 --> 00:12:24.090
You can add resources at
organization level, project

00:12:24.090 --> 00:12:27.090
level, or data set
level with BigQuery.

00:12:27.090 --> 00:12:29.680
All of your permissions
inherit from top to bottom.

00:12:29.680 --> 00:12:32.340
So if you add somebody in
admin at organization level,

00:12:32.340 --> 00:12:35.820
they'll automatically be
added to all of your projects

00:12:35.820 --> 00:12:38.419
and all of your data sets.

00:12:38.419 --> 00:12:40.710
On the right hand side, you
are seeing type of entities

00:12:40.710 --> 00:12:42.870
that can be added to BigQuery.

00:12:42.870 --> 00:12:44.730
So first three
options over there,

00:12:44.730 --> 00:12:47.160
users, groups, and
domains, can be added

00:12:47.160 --> 00:12:49.860
to any of these three levels.

00:12:49.860 --> 00:12:51.990
Public and authorized
views are only

00:12:51.990 --> 00:12:53.920
available at data set level.

00:12:53.920 --> 00:12:55.920
If you make any data
set public, that

00:12:55.920 --> 00:12:59.220
becomes available to any
GCP user in the world.

00:12:59.220 --> 00:13:00.720
So this option is
available when you

00:13:00.720 --> 00:13:02.490
want to open source
your data, or just

00:13:02.490 --> 00:13:05.090
make it available for
everyone to consume.

00:13:05.090 --> 00:13:06.810
Authorized views
basically give you

00:13:06.810 --> 00:13:12.030
an option to provide more
granular controls over how

00:13:12.030 --> 00:13:13.480
your users access data.

00:13:13.480 --> 00:13:16.830
So if you want to go
beyond data set level,

00:13:16.830 --> 00:13:19.350
you want to apply table
level or rule level access,

00:13:19.350 --> 00:13:22.030
you can use authorized
views for that.

00:13:22.030 --> 00:13:24.120
So now you have set up access.

00:13:24.120 --> 00:13:25.950
How do your users get started?

00:13:25.950 --> 00:13:28.380
So once your data is ingested,
good thing with BigQuery

00:13:28.380 --> 00:13:30.630
is, you don't need to
do any kind of tune up,

00:13:30.630 --> 00:13:33.580
any kind of indexing or
sizing up your environment.

00:13:33.580 --> 00:13:38.760
You can basically get started
just after ingesting your data.

00:13:38.760 --> 00:13:41.430
What your admins and
your DBAs can actually

00:13:41.430 --> 00:13:45.120
do to help your users out
is go to BigQuery console,

00:13:45.120 --> 00:13:47.210
write some sample
queries for users,

00:13:47.210 --> 00:13:51.270
share them, which will be
shared across all of your users

00:13:51.270 --> 00:13:52.055
in the project.

00:13:52.055 --> 00:13:54.180
You can make them private
as well, but sharing them

00:13:54.180 --> 00:13:57.420
will start your
analysts much faster.

00:13:57.420 --> 00:13:59.280
Also, BigQuery
supports standard SQL,

00:13:59.280 --> 00:14:01.140
which is compliant
with SQL 2011,

00:14:01.140 --> 00:14:05.980
so as long as your users know
SQL, they are good to go.

00:14:05.980 --> 00:14:08.730
Now, some of the organizations
have these central teams

00:14:08.730 --> 00:14:12.150
whose job is to manage
these curated data sets,

00:14:12.150 --> 00:14:15.840
and all other teams or
other call centers usually

00:14:15.840 --> 00:14:17.810
query those data and
create their own data

00:14:17.810 --> 00:14:19.590
set on top of that.

00:14:19.590 --> 00:14:22.590
Now, with BigQuery, you get
charged for storage separately

00:14:22.590 --> 00:14:24.240
and compute separately,
and you want

00:14:24.240 --> 00:14:27.780
to make sure that your storage
costs, or your central group,

00:14:27.780 --> 00:14:30.300
doesn't get charged for
querying by other users

00:14:30.300 --> 00:14:32.490
or other analysts or
other call centers.

00:14:32.490 --> 00:14:34.470
What you can do
is with BigQuery,

00:14:34.470 --> 00:14:37.340
create a central project which
will keep the security data

00:14:37.340 --> 00:14:40.379
sets, create separate projects
for your call centers,

00:14:40.379 --> 00:14:42.420
and they should use
different billing accounts so

00:14:42.420 --> 00:14:44.490
that what querying
costs they incur,

00:14:44.490 --> 00:14:46.020
it goes to their
billing accounts,

00:14:46.020 --> 00:14:49.710
and your central group just
pays for your storage costs.

00:14:49.710 --> 00:14:52.720
Also, Karthi just mentioned how
you can explore your data using

00:14:52.720 --> 00:14:54.360
BigQuery and
Datalab, but it also

00:14:54.360 --> 00:14:57.450
provides a lot of connectors
for tools like Tableau, Looker,

00:14:57.450 --> 00:15:00.480
and Data Studio to
visualize your date

00:15:00.480 --> 00:15:03.370
or run your reporting
workloads on top of it.

00:15:03.370 --> 00:15:05.070
Now your users are
consuming this data,

00:15:05.070 --> 00:15:08.450
so your admins need to
monitor your environment.

00:15:08.450 --> 00:15:11.580
BigQuery provides a lot
of metrics in Stackdriver

00:15:11.580 --> 00:15:14.034
itself to look at the
health of your environment.

00:15:14.034 --> 00:15:15.700
I have mentioned some
of these are here,

00:15:15.700 --> 00:15:17.949
which can be used by your
billing admins, your storage

00:15:17.949 --> 00:15:19.800
admins, or any kind
of resource admin

00:15:19.800 --> 00:15:22.590
to look at how your
environment is doing.

00:15:22.590 --> 00:15:27.270
They can also set up alerts and
notifications on these metrics

00:15:27.270 --> 00:15:30.240
so that whenever the
value of any metric

00:15:30.240 --> 00:15:33.100
goes above or below
that threshold,

00:15:33.100 --> 00:15:35.940
they can get notified for that.

00:15:35.940 --> 00:15:37.650
Let's talk about compliance.

00:15:37.650 --> 00:15:39.510
So from an auditing
perspective, BigQuery

00:15:39.510 --> 00:15:42.510
provides mainly two types of
logs, admin activity and data

00:15:42.510 --> 00:15:43.600
access activity.

00:15:43.600 --> 00:15:46.470
Admin activity keeps information
about schema changes,

00:15:46.470 --> 00:15:49.580
schema creation, data set
creation, table creation,

00:15:49.580 --> 00:15:50.700
et cetera.

00:15:50.700 --> 00:15:53.404
These logs are
available for 400 days.

00:15:53.404 --> 00:15:55.570
Data access activity logs
are available for 30 days,

00:15:55.570 --> 00:15:58.020
and that keeps
information about who

00:15:58.020 --> 00:16:00.420
is accessing what kind of
data or what kind of data

00:16:00.420 --> 00:16:02.610
is getting modified.

00:16:02.610 --> 00:16:05.370
If you want, if you
have a need, and you

00:16:05.370 --> 00:16:09.950
want to change how many
days the data gets retained,

00:16:09.950 --> 00:16:11.480
what you can do
is you can export

00:16:11.480 --> 00:16:15.950
these logs into Google Cloud
Storage or BigQuery itself.

00:16:15.950 --> 00:16:18.530
And you can set your
custom duration over there.

00:16:18.530 --> 00:16:22.920
If you want to look at these
logs on a real-time basis,

00:16:22.920 --> 00:16:26.057
you can always stream these
logs into Cloud Pub/Sub,

00:16:26.057 --> 00:16:27.890
and update your application
to Cloud Pub/Sub

00:16:27.890 --> 00:16:30.410
to be able to do that.

00:16:30.410 --> 00:16:33.180
Now your users are using it,
your admins are monitoring it,

00:16:33.180 --> 00:16:35.030
so of course, they'll
find new use case

00:16:35.030 --> 00:16:37.880
patterns and new information
that they did not

00:16:37.880 --> 00:16:39.140
know when they built it.

00:16:39.140 --> 00:16:43.380
So how do you optimize
based on what you know?

00:16:43.380 --> 00:16:45.450
So when it comes to
optimization with BigQuery,

00:16:45.450 --> 00:16:47.910
there are three major areas
which I would like to cover--

00:16:47.910 --> 00:16:50.040
storage, cost and compute.

00:16:50.040 --> 00:16:53.540
With storage, so a
lot of data warehouses

00:16:53.540 --> 00:16:56.400
have SLAs to keep data
for X number of days.

00:16:56.400 --> 00:16:59.670
That could be 100 days, 200
days, 300 days, whatever.

00:16:59.670 --> 00:17:02.730
And after that, team has
to remove that data either

00:17:02.730 --> 00:17:06.839
by building it in process
or doing it manually.

00:17:06.839 --> 00:17:08.369
With BigQuery,
what you can do is

00:17:08.369 --> 00:17:12.270
you can actually set expiration
time for any data set, table,

00:17:12.270 --> 00:17:16.530
or partition, and BigQuery will
automatically remove that data

00:17:16.530 --> 00:17:19.170
and clear up a storage for you.

00:17:19.170 --> 00:17:21.130
From a cost perspective,
one thing I say

00:17:21.130 --> 00:17:23.490
is, if you have already
loaded data in BigQuery

00:17:23.490 --> 00:17:26.670
and you are not modifying
it, just keep it in BigQuery.

00:17:26.670 --> 00:17:30.120
Because if you keep that data
in BigQuery [INAUDIBLE] table

00:17:30.120 --> 00:17:33.760
or partition for 90 days, and
don't change it for 90 days,

00:17:33.760 --> 00:17:36.810
BigQuery automatically charges
you much less for its storage

00:17:36.810 --> 00:17:39.469
costs because it considers
its a long term storage.

00:17:39.469 --> 00:17:41.010
Again, you don't
need to do anything.

00:17:41.010 --> 00:17:43.260
It just stays there, and
you'll get charged much less

00:17:43.260 --> 00:17:46.530
compared to how you will get
charged for active storage.

00:17:46.530 --> 00:17:48.232
Also, try to use
partition tables,

00:17:48.232 --> 00:17:50.190
because whenever you are
using partition tables

00:17:50.190 --> 00:17:52.830
and you use the right
filters with BigQuery,

00:17:52.830 --> 00:17:54.930
you scan much less
number of bytes

00:17:54.930 --> 00:17:56.700
and you get charged
much less compared

00:17:56.700 --> 00:17:59.130
to scanning the entire table.

00:17:59.130 --> 00:18:01.740
Also, sometimes you don't
know how big the table is,

00:18:01.740 --> 00:18:04.620
and you want to just get a feel
of how much your query will

00:18:04.620 --> 00:18:05.460
cost.

00:18:05.460 --> 00:18:07.140
Before running a
query, you can actually

00:18:07.140 --> 00:18:10.012
schedule a dry run for
query, and that way, BigQuery

00:18:10.012 --> 00:18:11.970
will give you some estimate
about how much it's

00:18:11.970 --> 00:18:13.130
going to cost you.

00:18:13.130 --> 00:18:16.860
So a good way for you to
just try before buying it.

00:18:16.860 --> 00:18:19.440
Also, if you are running
same query again and again,

00:18:19.440 --> 00:18:22.710
and I see that happening a lot
in your reporting workloads,

00:18:22.710 --> 00:18:25.591
BigQuery tries to read that
information from cache,

00:18:25.591 --> 00:18:27.840
and you don't get charged
for reading data from cache.

00:18:27.840 --> 00:18:32.150
So BigQuery tries
to read on its own.

00:18:32.150 --> 00:18:34.580
Now sometimes, you have
users who are new to BigQuery

00:18:34.580 --> 00:18:36.550
or new to organization,
and you really

00:18:36.550 --> 00:18:39.400
don't know how they are
going to work with BigQuery.

00:18:39.400 --> 00:18:43.180
And as this is a pay
as you go, your charges

00:18:43.180 --> 00:18:45.640
go up as soon as
they start using it.

00:18:45.640 --> 00:18:48.220
And you want to make sure, you
want to control how much data

00:18:48.220 --> 00:18:52.880
they access or how much cost
they incur on a daily basis.

00:18:52.880 --> 00:18:56.050
You can set custom quota
in BigQuery UI itself

00:18:56.050 --> 00:18:59.247
for any particular user
or any particular project,

00:18:59.247 --> 00:19:01.330
and that way, they will
never blow up your budget,

00:19:01.330 --> 00:19:03.550
or they will never
go out of the cost

00:19:03.550 --> 00:19:06.310
that you had decided
for them to use.

00:19:06.310 --> 00:19:09.070
Now, from compute
side, I always say

00:19:09.070 --> 00:19:10.740
start optimizing your queries.

00:19:10.740 --> 00:19:12.650
Make sure you use
the right queries.

00:19:12.650 --> 00:19:14.470
And what I mean by
that is, BigQuery

00:19:14.470 --> 00:19:16.030
is a columnar database.

00:19:16.030 --> 00:19:19.620
So that being said, don't try
to select [INAUDIBLE] with that.

00:19:19.620 --> 00:19:21.250
Try to use only
those columns which

00:19:21.250 --> 00:19:24.190
you need for your aggregation
or any kind of queries.

00:19:24.190 --> 00:19:26.860
Try to use partition tables
and write partition filters

00:19:26.860 --> 00:19:27.850
with that.

00:19:27.850 --> 00:19:30.700
Also, try to denormalize
tables whenever you can,

00:19:30.700 --> 00:19:33.070
because cost of
denormalization and storage

00:19:33.070 --> 00:19:37.150
is much less compared
to cost of join.

00:19:37.150 --> 00:19:39.190
Also, as you might
know, BigQuery usually

00:19:39.190 --> 00:19:40.510
charges for pay as you go.

00:19:40.510 --> 00:19:42.310
That's the by default
option, and what

00:19:42.310 --> 00:19:46.060
I mean by that is, whenever you
create any project, by default,

00:19:46.060 --> 00:19:48.670
you get up to 2,000 slots.

00:19:48.670 --> 00:19:50.170
And what I mean by
slots is, you can

00:19:50.170 --> 00:19:51.760
think of slot as
a computing unit

00:19:51.760 --> 00:19:53.280
that you get with BigQuery.

00:19:53.280 --> 00:19:55.810
You get up to 2,000
computing cores

00:19:55.810 --> 00:19:59.330
or computing units with BigQuery
by default for your project.

00:19:59.330 --> 00:20:01.830
So what happens is, if
you are running one query,

00:20:01.830 --> 00:20:03.760
it can take up to 2,000 slots.

00:20:03.760 --> 00:20:05.260
If you are running
multiple queries,

00:20:05.260 --> 00:20:07.870
that can also take
up to 2,000 slots.

00:20:07.870 --> 00:20:09.820
So if you have more
important queries

00:20:09.820 --> 00:20:12.070
and less important queries
in the same project, what

00:20:12.070 --> 00:20:14.420
you can do is, you can actually
set those less important

00:20:14.420 --> 00:20:17.249
queries and run them
as batch queries.

00:20:17.249 --> 00:20:18.790
That way, they'll
get de-prioritized.

00:20:18.790 --> 00:20:21.070
They will still finish, but
they'll get de-prioritized

00:20:21.070 --> 00:20:22.825
and your actually
important query,

00:20:22.825 --> 00:20:25.480
which has strict
[INAUDIBLE] will run first.

00:20:25.480 --> 00:20:28.240
Now, you might be coming from
some large organizations,

00:20:28.240 --> 00:20:30.970
and you might have very
large analytics workloads.

00:20:30.970 --> 00:20:33.397
In that scenario, you can
go to the second option

00:20:33.397 --> 00:20:35.230
that I have mentioned,
which is fixed slots.

00:20:35.230 --> 00:20:39.610
A lot of the organizations have
very high analytics workloads,

00:20:39.610 --> 00:20:42.520
and they can actually come to
us and buy additional number

00:20:42.520 --> 00:20:43.390
of fixed slots.

00:20:43.390 --> 00:20:45.910
So by default, you get
2,000, but you can buy up

00:20:45.910 --> 00:20:48.970
to any number, and
that will be a fixed

00:20:48.970 --> 00:20:53.170
number of slots for your
organization or your project.

00:20:53.170 --> 00:20:56.830
With fixed number of slots, it
also controls the cost in a way

00:20:56.830 --> 00:20:59.770
that you always know how much
your monthly compute cost is

00:20:59.770 --> 00:21:02.020
going to be with BigQuery.

00:21:02.020 --> 00:21:04.830
So because once you buy
a fixed number of slots,

00:21:04.830 --> 00:21:08.320
there is no pay as you go, you
can run any number of queries

00:21:08.320 --> 00:21:10.800
and you'll still get billed
for how many slots you

00:21:10.800 --> 00:21:12.250
have purchased.

00:21:12.250 --> 00:21:14.890
Also, one option which
becomes available,

00:21:14.890 --> 00:21:17.050
and we call it
hierarchical slots,

00:21:17.050 --> 00:21:19.970
is something that
comes with fixed slots.

00:21:19.970 --> 00:21:21.720
And I'm going to give
you an example of it

00:21:21.720 --> 00:21:23.770
in the next slide.

00:21:23.770 --> 00:21:27.360
So let's assume you have four
projects, R&amp;D, dashboards, ETL,

00:21:27.360 --> 00:21:28.599
and ad hoc.

00:21:28.599 --> 00:21:30.390
Bottom two projects
are important projects.

00:21:30.390 --> 00:21:31.530
They are strict SLAs.

00:21:31.530 --> 00:21:34.230
You want to make sure they
finish on time, all the jobs

00:21:34.230 --> 00:21:35.640
for them finish on time.

00:21:35.640 --> 00:21:38.520
ETL project is mainly a
playground area or sandbox

00:21:38.520 --> 00:21:41.760
for your new users where
they just want to try things.

00:21:41.760 --> 00:21:43.980
You don't want to incur
any additional cost

00:21:43.980 --> 00:21:45.750
for the ad hoc projects.

00:21:45.750 --> 00:21:48.030
If you buy a fixed number
of slots, what you can do

00:21:48.030 --> 00:21:50.170
is you can configure
this [INAUDIBLE] project,

00:21:50.170 --> 00:21:53.970
ad hoc project, in a way
that it uses resources only

00:21:53.970 --> 00:21:56.430
when they are available
on bottom three projects.

00:21:56.430 --> 00:22:00.780
So any of the projects below
have any slots available,

00:22:00.780 --> 00:22:03.780
this ad hoc project can take
those slots from those projects

00:22:03.780 --> 00:22:05.730
and start using those resources.

00:22:05.730 --> 00:22:09.990
So that way, you can share or
prioritize or de-prioritize

00:22:09.990 --> 00:22:12.330
your projects as
well in BigQuery,

00:22:12.330 --> 00:22:14.850
but you need to have fixed
number of slots for that.

00:22:14.850 --> 00:22:18.330
So there are some ways you can
optimize your cost or storage

00:22:18.330 --> 00:22:20.690
or compute in BigQuery.

00:22:20.690 --> 00:22:22.890
Now, before I ask
Tomasz to come on stage

00:22:22.890 --> 00:22:24.810
and present his
Ocado case, I'm just

00:22:24.810 --> 00:22:27.490
going to summarize what
we have discussed so far.

00:22:27.490 --> 00:22:29.940
So as Karthi mentioned,
BigQuery works seamlessly

00:22:29.940 --> 00:22:30.630
with data lake.

00:22:30.630 --> 00:22:33.660
You can explore your data, you
can do mining and exploration

00:22:33.660 --> 00:22:35.220
right over there.

00:22:35.220 --> 00:22:37.440
Once you have built
your data mart,

00:22:37.440 --> 00:22:39.090
you can centrally
manage your resources

00:22:39.090 --> 00:22:40.860
through Identity and
Access Management,

00:22:40.860 --> 00:22:43.950
and you can monitor
them using Stackdriver.

00:22:43.950 --> 00:22:46.970
When it comes to flexibility
and compute needs,

00:22:46.970 --> 00:22:50.010
BigQuery gives you a really
great flexibility regarding

00:22:50.010 --> 00:22:51.640
what you want to do when.

00:22:51.640 --> 00:22:54.660
So if you need more compute,
you can buy additional number

00:22:54.660 --> 00:22:55.680
of fixed slots.

00:22:55.680 --> 00:22:59.920
If you just need more storage,
just keep your default option,

00:22:59.920 --> 00:23:01.860
and you'll be fine.

00:23:01.860 --> 00:23:04.650
Last thing I wanted to
mention was, you can always

00:23:04.650 --> 00:23:06.540
share with BigQuery.

00:23:06.540 --> 00:23:09.270
And the way it works, you can
always share and calibrate

00:23:09.270 --> 00:23:12.900
your data, compute as well as
your queries across your users

00:23:12.900 --> 00:23:15.265
in your organization
very seamlessly.

00:23:15.265 --> 00:23:17.640
That being said, I'm going to
ask Tomasz to come on stage

00:23:17.640 --> 00:23:21.366
and present how Ocado
is using BigQuery.

00:23:21.366 --> 00:23:22.990
Also, one announcement
I wanted to make

00:23:22.990 --> 00:23:25.210
is, this session is
Dory enabled, which

00:23:25.210 --> 00:23:28.954
is our live questions platform,
so you can go on your app

00:23:28.954 --> 00:23:30.370
and if you have
any questions, you

00:23:30.370 --> 00:23:34.930
can put those questions in
this session's questionnaires.

00:23:34.930 --> 00:23:35.972
Thank you so much.

00:23:35.972 --> 00:23:40.394
[APPLAUSE]

00:23:40.394 --> 00:23:41.810
