00:23:43.920 --> 00:23:45.380
My name is Tomasz Stradomski.

00:23:45.380 --> 00:23:47.630
I'm leading data
engineering at Ocado.

00:23:47.630 --> 00:23:49.730
You have heard quite a
lot of useful information

00:23:49.730 --> 00:23:52.080
about products that are
available in Google Cloud

00:23:52.080 --> 00:23:55.700
Platform, and how you can
use them to store and analyze

00:23:55.700 --> 00:23:57.520
your data.

00:23:57.520 --> 00:23:59.700
Now I would like to tell
you how in Ocado, we

00:23:59.700 --> 00:24:01.650
combine those
components together

00:24:01.650 --> 00:24:05.760
to build our data lake.

00:24:05.760 --> 00:24:07.920
Not sure if you have
heard about Ocado before,

00:24:07.920 --> 00:24:10.200
but let me just
quickly introduce

00:24:10.200 --> 00:24:11.850
to you what we are doing.

00:24:11.850 --> 00:24:15.990
So we are the world's largest
online only supermarket.

00:24:15.990 --> 00:24:18.960
We deliver more or
less 50,000 products

00:24:18.960 --> 00:24:23.430
to more than 600,000
active customers in UK.

00:24:23.430 --> 00:24:27.450
But what is important, we
build our solution in house.

00:24:27.450 --> 00:24:31.020
For example, robotics
in our warehouses,

00:24:31.020 --> 00:24:35.160
front end application, back
end systems, everything.

00:24:35.160 --> 00:24:39.160
The heart of Ocado are
highly automated warehouses,

00:24:39.160 --> 00:24:42.210
which we call CFC, Customer
Fulfillment Center.

00:24:42.210 --> 00:24:46.290
So you can imagine that on the
grid of size of football field,

00:24:46.290 --> 00:24:49.380
there are hundreds of bots
moving, carrying some products,

00:24:49.380 --> 00:24:51.090
doing some operations
just to fulfill

00:24:51.090 --> 00:24:53.760
orders of our customers.

00:24:53.760 --> 00:24:56.460
But Ocado is not only CFC.

00:24:56.460 --> 00:25:00.390
We created e-commerce
end-to-end solution.

00:25:00.390 --> 00:25:03.480
That of course, includes
delivery systems,

00:25:03.480 --> 00:25:06.600
customer center
systems, so basically,

00:25:06.600 --> 00:25:09.570
everything we needed to
succeed in this business.

00:25:09.570 --> 00:25:12.000
As you can imagine,
we collect quite a lot

00:25:12.000 --> 00:25:14.140
of data from those systems.

00:25:14.140 --> 00:25:16.930
And we use them to
leverage our business.

00:25:16.930 --> 00:25:20.190
For example, we have
recommendation systems,

00:25:20.190 --> 00:25:23.400
fraud detection,
automatic classification

00:25:23.400 --> 00:25:27.020
of emails that is using
deep network, deep learning.

00:25:30.310 --> 00:25:32.050
But this is just
half of our story.

00:25:32.050 --> 00:25:36.180
So on one side, Ocado is
the online supermarket.

00:25:36.180 --> 00:25:38.790
But on the other side,
let's for a second imagine

00:25:38.790 --> 00:25:40.290
that you are a
traditional retailer,

00:25:40.290 --> 00:25:42.840
and you'd like to go online.

00:25:42.840 --> 00:25:45.630
This opportunity is called
Ocado Smart Platforms.

00:25:45.630 --> 00:25:48.540
So the idea is that we
are selling our technology

00:25:48.540 --> 00:25:49.752
as a service.

00:25:49.752 --> 00:25:51.210
At the moment, we
have the pleasure

00:25:51.210 --> 00:25:57.090
to work with several retailers
in Europe and North America.

00:25:57.090 --> 00:26:01.890
To be able to start new
environments for new retailers,

00:26:01.890 --> 00:26:04.230
we moved our solution to cloud.

00:26:04.230 --> 00:26:05.760
And the process
of moving to cloud

00:26:05.760 --> 00:26:07.500
gave us quite a
unique opportunity

00:26:07.500 --> 00:26:10.590
to rethink and
redesign our systems.

00:26:10.590 --> 00:26:14.250
And that also includes
data architecture.

00:26:14.250 --> 00:26:16.490
So before moving to
cloud, we've been

00:26:16.490 --> 00:26:18.260
working with some
on-prem solutions

00:26:18.260 --> 00:26:22.010
like [? Oracle, ?] Greenplum,
different on-prem file

00:26:22.010 --> 00:26:24.050
storages.

00:26:24.050 --> 00:26:29.720
And it is a challenge because
you need to plan your capacity,

00:26:29.720 --> 00:26:32.080
what data is most
important for you,

00:26:32.080 --> 00:26:33.710
what to do if you
are running out

00:26:33.710 --> 00:26:37.280
of storage, how to implement
your attention policing.

00:26:37.280 --> 00:26:40.550
So we didn't want to
care about that anymore.

00:26:40.550 --> 00:26:43.070
So we wanted to
build a solution that

00:26:43.070 --> 00:26:46.620
is able to store all
the data we need.

00:26:46.620 --> 00:26:49.610
Secondly, we wanted to have
the data processing so easy

00:26:49.610 --> 00:26:52.670
easy that everyone in the
organization can do it.

00:26:52.670 --> 00:26:55.880
In most organizations, there
are some dedicated data teams

00:26:55.880 --> 00:27:00.320
like big data, data engineering,
machine learning teams.

00:27:00.320 --> 00:27:02.030
We of course also
have such teams,

00:27:02.030 --> 00:27:05.480
but they are mostly working
on toolkits, best practices,

00:27:05.480 --> 00:27:07.010
and enablement.

00:27:07.010 --> 00:27:09.050
And the idea is
that we would like

00:27:09.050 --> 00:27:11.360
to enable everyone
in the organization

00:27:11.360 --> 00:27:13.380
to be able to process the data.

00:27:13.380 --> 00:27:17.150
And to do that, you need a
really simple and coherent

00:27:17.150 --> 00:27:19.350
solution.

00:27:19.350 --> 00:27:22.230
So we run a couple of proofs
of concept, and at some point,

00:27:22.230 --> 00:27:25.160
we tested BigQuery.

00:27:25.160 --> 00:27:29.150
You have heard quite
a lot about BigQuery,

00:27:29.150 --> 00:27:32.900
but let me just quickly tell
you what was important to us.

00:27:32.900 --> 00:27:35.880
So first of all, it's
extremely easy to use.

00:27:35.880 --> 00:27:38.370
You don't need to
worry about DP tuning.

00:27:38.370 --> 00:27:40.590
So you don't need
to maintain indexes,

00:27:40.590 --> 00:27:45.560
materialized views, hundreds
of parameters, tune memory

00:27:45.560 --> 00:27:47.510
structures, storage structures.

00:27:47.510 --> 00:27:51.200
You just load your data
and you can query it.

00:27:51.200 --> 00:27:52.820
In BigQuery, you
pay for two things,

00:27:52.820 --> 00:27:56.400
for storage and for
querying your data.

00:27:56.400 --> 00:27:59.000
And it depends on
the volume of data

00:27:59.000 --> 00:28:00.590
you, of course, are scanning.

00:28:00.590 --> 00:28:01.760
But it's quite transparent.

00:28:01.760 --> 00:28:03.560
You have good control over it.

00:28:03.560 --> 00:28:05.570
It's easy to enable
your billings,

00:28:05.570 --> 00:28:08.690
and there are some things
you can do to reduce

00:28:08.690 --> 00:28:10.260
the cost of using the solution.

00:28:10.260 --> 00:28:13.040
And it has been
covered by Dhaval.

00:28:13.040 --> 00:28:15.650
It works quite well with
Google Cloud Storage.

00:28:15.650 --> 00:28:18.890
So basically, if you have
a lot of unstructured data,

00:28:18.890 --> 00:28:21.440
it's probably a good
idea to store it

00:28:21.440 --> 00:28:23.630
on Google Cloud Storage.

00:28:23.630 --> 00:28:27.470
But there are some products
that you can use in Google Cloud

00:28:27.470 --> 00:28:32.030
to extract structure information
and store it in BigQuery.

00:28:32.030 --> 00:28:34.530
In general, we don't have
a lot of unstructured data,

00:28:34.530 --> 00:28:36.710
so we are storing
everything in BigQuery.

00:28:36.710 --> 00:28:40.720
So this is our main
data lake solution.

00:28:40.720 --> 00:28:45.007
Maybe the most important
part, its scale is endless.

00:28:45.007 --> 00:28:47.090
So basically, you can
imagine that under the hood,

00:28:47.090 --> 00:28:50.660
you have the processing
power of Google data centers,

00:28:50.660 --> 00:28:56.130
with both great computation,
colossal file system, Jupyter

00:28:56.130 --> 00:28:57.050
network.

00:28:57.050 --> 00:29:00.500
So almost no way to
exhaust those resources.

00:29:00.500 --> 00:29:02.450
And the last thing,
quite important to us

00:29:02.450 --> 00:29:03.570
is data locality.

00:29:03.570 --> 00:29:06.410
We at the moment
operate mostly in EU.

00:29:06.410 --> 00:29:09.519
That's why we wanted to
have our data stored in EU.

00:29:09.519 --> 00:29:11.560
And this is something that
BigQuery gives you out

00:29:11.560 --> 00:29:12.330
of the box.

00:29:12.330 --> 00:29:13.800
You can choose the location.

00:29:13.800 --> 00:29:17.020
It can be US, Europe,
Asia, whatever you need.

00:29:22.180 --> 00:29:24.210
So we decided that
BigQuery is going

00:29:24.210 --> 00:29:27.450
to be the brain
of our data lake,

00:29:27.450 --> 00:29:30.120
but we needed a simple
approach of loading

00:29:30.120 --> 00:29:32.580
the data into BigQuery.

00:29:32.580 --> 00:29:34.420
And we tried even sourcing.

00:29:34.420 --> 00:29:37.980
So just to remind
you, our applications

00:29:37.980 --> 00:29:41.850
are running in the cloud, and we
use microservices architecture.

00:29:41.850 --> 00:29:45.330
So those microservices are
doing some business operations,

00:29:45.330 --> 00:29:47.790
and that of course
generates some data.

00:29:47.790 --> 00:29:51.180
So idea is that we pack
this data into event

00:29:51.180 --> 00:29:53.190
and send it to the cloud.

00:29:53.190 --> 00:29:57.480
So for example, the
customer makes an order,

00:29:57.480 --> 00:29:59.340
there is an event sent.

00:29:59.340 --> 00:30:01.210
The order is picked
or delivered,

00:30:01.210 --> 00:30:02.680
there is an event sent.

00:30:02.680 --> 00:30:06.060
The payment is taken from
the account, event sent.

00:30:06.060 --> 00:30:09.510
And all those events are
sent to the stream in cloud.

00:30:09.510 --> 00:30:12.190
We use Kinesis at the moment.

00:30:12.190 --> 00:30:16.230
Now, you may ask why we are
not using Pub/Sub from Google,

00:30:16.230 --> 00:30:17.910
and that's because
when we started

00:30:17.910 --> 00:30:23.190
working on this architecture,
Pub/Sub was not available.

00:30:23.190 --> 00:30:27.210
OK, so all our events are sent
to the stream in the cloud.

00:30:27.210 --> 00:30:30.260
And from there, we
stream it into BigQuery.

00:30:30.260 --> 00:30:35.670
So we created a real-time
application on Apache Beam.

00:30:35.670 --> 00:30:37.940
It's running on Dataflow.

00:30:37.940 --> 00:30:40.010
And its main
responsibility is just

00:30:40.010 --> 00:30:43.820
reading the events from Kinesis
and streaming it to BigQuery,

00:30:43.820 --> 00:30:46.340
so this has been
covered by Karthi.

00:30:46.340 --> 00:30:51.290
But at the moment, we are
using streaming approach only.

00:30:51.290 --> 00:30:54.280
That way, all the
data from our systems,

00:30:54.280 --> 00:30:58.070
the data is available in our
lake in a matter of seconds.

00:31:01.920 --> 00:31:05.030
Why we decided to run event
processor on data flow.

00:31:05.030 --> 00:31:06.530
To be honest, at
the very beginning,

00:31:06.530 --> 00:31:09.110
we tried Sparks streaming.

00:31:09.110 --> 00:31:12.270
It was working pretty well,
but we had two main issues.

00:31:12.270 --> 00:31:16.070
So first of all, we
had problems with HA.

00:31:16.070 --> 00:31:19.550
So basically, what to do
if your workers are failing

00:31:19.550 --> 00:31:22.100
or master node is
failing, and especially

00:31:22.100 --> 00:31:24.410
what to do to make sure
that in case of failure,

00:31:24.410 --> 00:31:27.900
we are not losing any events.

00:31:27.900 --> 00:31:30.590
And secondly, we
needed auto scanning.

00:31:30.590 --> 00:31:33.600
Our workload varies,
so at some point,

00:31:33.600 --> 00:31:35.510
we are sending
thousands of events

00:31:35.510 --> 00:31:40.460
per minute, a little bit
later, hundreds of thousands.

00:31:40.460 --> 00:31:43.970
And those problems have been
solved by Dataflow for us.

00:31:47.190 --> 00:31:51.210
As mentioned before, we
are using structured data.

00:31:51.210 --> 00:31:53.220
And to have a good
control over what

00:31:53.220 --> 00:31:57.730
we are sending to our lake, we
introduced another component,

00:31:57.730 --> 00:31:59.490
which we call Data Registry.

00:31:59.490 --> 00:32:02.490
It's an App Engine
application in which

00:32:02.490 --> 00:32:06.120
every type of the event
that enters our system

00:32:06.120 --> 00:32:07.860
must be registered.

00:32:07.860 --> 00:32:11.370
By registered, I mean the user
needs to define the schema,

00:32:11.370 --> 00:32:14.670
so what are the fields
inside this event.

00:32:14.670 --> 00:32:17.550
Those fields are
mapped into columns

00:32:17.550 --> 00:32:21.720
in BigQuery tables,
what are the data types,

00:32:21.720 --> 00:32:25.050
and submitted data information,
like event name, event group,

00:32:25.050 --> 00:32:27.840
which we used to
route this event

00:32:27.840 --> 00:32:32.350
into a particular
location in our data lake.

00:32:32.350 --> 00:32:34.530
This is also our
data governance tool,

00:32:34.530 --> 00:32:37.410
because every single piece
of information that enters

00:32:37.410 --> 00:32:39.840
our data lake is stored here.

00:32:39.840 --> 00:32:45.180
So basically, you can run some
text search to see your data,

00:32:45.180 --> 00:32:49.170
from where it's sent and where
it is routed in BigQuery.

00:32:51.900 --> 00:32:54.920
So let's talk about
our data model.

00:32:54.920 --> 00:32:56.600
So as mentioned,
we have our events

00:32:56.600 --> 00:32:58.460
which we call row events.

00:32:58.460 --> 00:33:02.090
And what is important,
this is the private area

00:33:02.090 --> 00:33:05.420
for the team that is producing
this particular piece

00:33:05.420 --> 00:33:08.200
of information.

00:33:08.200 --> 00:33:10.510
No one as an
organization is allowed

00:33:10.510 --> 00:33:12.810
to read row events,
which are basically

00:33:12.810 --> 00:33:15.220
mapped to BigQuery tables.

00:33:15.220 --> 00:33:18.820
To expose this data to the
rest of the organization,

00:33:18.820 --> 00:33:22.310
we created a kind
of data level API.

00:33:22.310 --> 00:33:26.290
So we introduced so-called data
objects and derived objects.

00:33:29.110 --> 00:33:31.970
So for creation of the data
objects, in most cases,

00:33:31.970 --> 00:33:34.170
this is just a view
or physical table

00:33:34.170 --> 00:33:38.000
that is refreshed a
couple of times a day.

00:33:38.000 --> 00:33:40.460
Our responsible data
producers, so teams

00:33:40.460 --> 00:33:42.980
that are producing
this information,

00:33:42.980 --> 00:33:45.980
so basically this is how we
enable the whole organization

00:33:45.980 --> 00:33:46.910
data processing.

00:33:46.910 --> 00:33:49.900
So teams are responsible
for creating the views.

00:33:49.900 --> 00:33:54.710
The views are combining those
information from microservices

00:33:54.710 --> 00:33:57.680
by doing unions, joins,
deduplication, aggregation,

00:33:57.680 --> 00:34:02.150
whatever makes sense, to create
a kind of business entity,

00:34:02.150 --> 00:34:04.580
meaningful business entity.

00:34:04.580 --> 00:34:07.490
To do that, we run
quite a lot of workshops

00:34:07.490 --> 00:34:10.730
to show people how to use
BigQuery, demonstrate some

00:34:10.730 --> 00:34:13.000
of the most important
aspects of BigQuery,

00:34:13.000 --> 00:34:17.540
and teach them how to
process the data in general.

00:34:17.540 --> 00:34:20.130
So data object and derived
object are quite similar.

00:34:20.130 --> 00:34:24.020
The main difference is that
derived object may blend data

00:34:24.020 --> 00:34:26.960
from multiple systems, for
example, order management

00:34:26.960 --> 00:34:28.730
data with payments data.

00:34:28.730 --> 00:34:31.460
So this is a kind of
data mart that fits

00:34:31.460 --> 00:34:33.800
some specific business purpose.

00:34:33.800 --> 00:34:35.810
And derived objects
we are sometimes

00:34:35.810 --> 00:34:37.850
exporting to Google
Cloud Storage,

00:34:37.850 --> 00:34:41.389
and this is how we make it
available for our retailers

00:34:41.389 --> 00:34:44.330
to be downloaded and analyzed.

00:34:44.330 --> 00:34:48.770
So just to sum up, events,
this is private implementation

00:34:48.770 --> 00:34:50.000
of our data.

00:34:50.000 --> 00:34:52.170
Internal API, data objects--

00:34:52.170 --> 00:34:55.250
so this is how we are
exposing the data internally.

00:34:55.250 --> 00:34:57.800
And external API
derived object, they

00:34:57.800 --> 00:35:01.280
are also shared with the
retailers we work with.

00:35:04.300 --> 00:35:07.380
To do this transformation
from row events to data object

00:35:07.380 --> 00:35:12.580
and to derived object, we needed
a kind of workflow scheduler

00:35:12.580 --> 00:35:14.140
orchestration engine.

00:35:14.140 --> 00:35:17.350
And a couple of years
ago, we couldn't

00:35:17.350 --> 00:35:19.730
find anything that
satisfied our needs,

00:35:19.730 --> 00:35:21.490
so we created our own solution.

00:35:21.490 --> 00:35:23.260
We call it Query Manager.

00:35:23.260 --> 00:35:24.760
So this is the
application where you

00:35:24.760 --> 00:35:28.480
can create a task like execute
a query, create a view,

00:35:28.480 --> 00:35:32.460
extract some data
to Google Storage.

00:35:32.460 --> 00:35:36.480
You can combine those tasks in
workflows and schedule them.

00:35:36.480 --> 00:35:38.830
But at the moment,
there is a new component

00:35:38.830 --> 00:35:41.220
in Google Cloud called
Google Cloud Composer,

00:35:41.220 --> 00:35:43.770
and it basically solves the
similar business problem.

00:35:43.770 --> 00:35:45.690
So it's a workflow
orchestration engine.

00:35:49.240 --> 00:35:52.800
OK, so we have three levels
of data classification.

00:35:52.800 --> 00:35:56.010
First is the type of the
object that I just mentioned.

00:35:56.010 --> 00:35:58.140
So row events, data objects
and derived objects.

00:35:58.140 --> 00:36:01.350
Second is business
or subject area.

00:36:01.350 --> 00:36:05.670
So what is the content
in this particular table,

00:36:05.670 --> 00:36:12.550
and do we figure out a number
of areas, this is just a sample,

00:36:12.550 --> 00:36:13.410
with sub-areas.

00:36:13.410 --> 00:36:16.600
So we classify all
our data that way.

00:36:16.600 --> 00:36:18.090
So for example,
if you would like

00:36:18.090 --> 00:36:20.580
to take a look on some
payment information,

00:36:20.580 --> 00:36:23.715
you can find them in the
area payment details.

00:36:26.570 --> 00:36:30.140
And the third level
of classification

00:36:30.140 --> 00:36:32.370
relates to security.

00:36:32.370 --> 00:36:33.750
We have three levels.

00:36:33.750 --> 00:36:37.100
Public, so this is the kind of
data that can be open sourced.

00:36:37.100 --> 00:36:39.000
At the moment, we
are not doing that,

00:36:39.000 --> 00:36:41.570
but the platform
is ready for that.

00:36:41.570 --> 00:36:45.890
Second level is restricted, so
everyone in the organization

00:36:45.890 --> 00:36:48.590
should have access to
this data if needed.

00:36:48.590 --> 00:36:51.950
And confidential, this
is a bit more secured.

00:36:51.950 --> 00:36:54.650
That's because this
kind of information

00:36:54.650 --> 00:36:56.960
may contain some
sensitive information,

00:36:56.960 --> 00:37:00.170
like PII data of our customers.

00:37:00.170 --> 00:37:03.110
And combining those three
classifications together,

00:37:03.110 --> 00:37:07.790
we created a convention of how
we are naming our data sets.

00:37:07.790 --> 00:37:12.320
So data set in BigQuery is
an aggregation of DB objects,

00:37:12.320 --> 00:37:13.760
of use end tables.

00:37:13.760 --> 00:37:17.120
And it has been mentioned by
Dhaval but just me repeating,

00:37:17.120 --> 00:37:19.850
you grant accesses
on data set level.

00:37:19.850 --> 00:37:22.670
So basically, you grant
access to every single table

00:37:22.670 --> 00:37:26.966
and view there is in
this particular data set.

00:37:26.966 --> 00:37:28.340
Let's take a look
on the example.

00:37:28.340 --> 00:37:31.760
For example, this second
data set, order details.

00:37:31.760 --> 00:37:33.530
It means that we
have information

00:37:33.530 --> 00:37:35.900
about orders there, data.

00:37:35.900 --> 00:37:38.210
It's not derived, so
the information are not

00:37:38.210 --> 00:37:40.040
blended with different systems.

00:37:40.040 --> 00:37:41.750
It's purely about orders.

00:37:41.750 --> 00:37:44.030
And restricted,
so no PII inside,

00:37:44.030 --> 00:37:46.820
everyone in the
organization can use it.

00:37:46.820 --> 00:37:51.360
So this is by just taking a
look on the name of the data

00:37:51.360 --> 00:37:56.115
set you know more or
less what is inside.

00:37:56.115 --> 00:37:59.340
Yeah, so this is basically
how we are governing our data,

00:37:59.340 --> 00:38:02.440
how we are loading
the data to our lake,

00:38:02.440 --> 00:38:06.670
and how we are controlling
and transforming our data.

00:38:09.720 --> 00:38:13.170
If you are interested in
building data lakes on GCP,

00:38:13.170 --> 00:38:17.100
I would recommend taking a
look on materials linked here.

00:38:17.100 --> 00:38:19.380
For example, article
or some quick start

00:38:19.380 --> 00:38:22.770
guides if you are not very
familiar with BigQuery.

00:38:22.770 --> 00:38:27.200
There is quite a lot of
